             :attribution "U.S. Geological Survey, Mineral Commodity Summaries 2026 (CC0 1.0)"}
   :rows    {:source 8886 :committed 214}
   :filter
   "Commodity in {Cobalt, Copper, Gallium, Graphite (Natural), Lead, Lithium, Nickel, Rare Earths,
Silicon}; Section contains \"World\" (the world-production sections, not the United-States salient
statistics); Statistics = \"Production\"; Country not in {World total, Other countries, blank};
Year and Value both parse as numbers. Values are stripped of thousands separators and converted to
metric tons (kilograms x 0.001, thousand metric tons x 1000). Where USGS lists a mine and a
refinery figure under the same commodity/country/year, the larger is kept. Country names
normalised: \"Congo (Kinshasa)\" -> \"Democratic Republic of Congo\", \"Korea, Republic of\" ->
\"South Korea\", \"Korea, North\" -> \"North Korea\", \"Burma\" -> \"Myanmar\". Sorted by
(Commodity, Country, year)."
   :columns {:passthrough ["Commodity" "Country" "Unit"]
             :computed    {"year"              "Year, parsed"
                           "production_tonnes" "Value, comma-stripped and unit-normalised to metric tons"}
             :dropped     ["Is critical mineral 2025" "MCS chapter" "Section" "Statistics"
                           "Statistics_detail" "Notes" "Other notes"]}
   :coarse-column "Commodity"
   :coarse-values ["Cobalt" "Copper" "Gallium" "Graphite (Natural)" "Lead"
                   "Lithium" "Nickel" "Rare Earths" "Silicon"]
   :notes
   "USGS's `Is critical mineral 2025` column was dropped for the same reason as MET's Department:
it holds two values (Yes/No) and would have out-ranked `Commodity` as the coarse column. `Unit` is
kept although constant, so the table says what its numbers are in; a one-value column cannot be a
cat-col, so it is harmless.
The source file is cp1252-encoded, not UTF-8 — its Section values contain an em dash that decodes
to U+FFFD if you assume UTF-8 — and its Value cells carry thousands separators (\"226,000\"), which
loci's ->val would leave as a string rather than parse as a number. Both are handled here."
   :entangles ["cobalt" "gallium" "china" "democratic republic of congo" "lead"
               "united states" "silicon" "rare earths"]}

