   :rows    {:source 2978 :committed 445}
   :filter
   "int_rt = IRT_M3 (the three-month rate, the one series comparable across all these economies);
geo in the ten with IRT_M3 coverage {CZ DK EA HU JP PL RO SE UK US}; TIME_PERIOD between 2020-01
and 2024-12 inclusive; non-blank OBS_VALUE. Geo codes expanded to names so `united states` is
findable as itself. The source row count is of the API response for startPeriod=2020-01, not of the
whole dataflow. Coverage is genuinely ragged — Japan ends 2023-01, the UK 2024-03, the US 2024-09 —
and the holes are left in. Sorted by (month, country)."
   :columns {:passthrough []
             :computed    {"month"    "TIME_PERIOD, renamed — `timeseries?` keys on this exact name"
                           "country"  "geo code expanded to a country name"
                           "rate_pct" "OBS_VALUE"}}
   :coarse-column "country"
   :coarse-values ["Czechia" "Denmark" "Euro area" "Hungary" "Japan" "Poland"
                   "Romania" "Sweden" "United Kingdom" "United States"]
   :notes
   "This is the ONLY table with a `month` column, and so the only one that molds as Over time or
Headline. The other four are annual and were left annual on purpose: `timeseries?` keys on a column
literally named :month, so annual data never charts however obviously temporal it is, and that gap
is a finding to see rather than paper over.
The BIS's central-bank policy rate dataset (WS_CBPOL) is the better fit for the word \"policy\" and
was rejected on licence — see :rejected below. Eurostat's three-month money-market rate is what an
attributable source offers, and it tracks policy closely: the US series runs 0.22% in 2021-01 to
5.36% in 2024-08.
Known wart, and not a defect in the data: `line` looks for a series column among #{:tenure-band
:dept :channel :region :stage}, and `country` is not one of them, so Over time draws all ten
economies as a single interleaved line."
   :entangles ["united states" "japan" "euro area" "united kingdom"]}]

